    { math: '4 · The data stack', note: 'Farm management software fuses soil, yield, weather, imagery and machinery data into decisions (the data-science lesson\'s pipeline, agrarian edition); the open question is data ownership — who owns the yield map, farmer or platform, is agriculture\'s quiet antitrust fight.' },
  ],
};

const vrCfg = {
  title: 'Worked example: NDVI readings and the economic optimum for nitrogen',
  steps: [
    { math: 'NDVI = (NIR − red) / (NIR + red)', note: 'healthy leaves absorb red (photosynthesis) and reflect near-infrared (cell structure); the index runs 0 = bare ground to ~0.8 = dense canopy.' },
    { math: 'readings: water ≈ 0 or below; bare soil ≈ 0.1–0.2; stressed crop ≈ 0.3–0.5; thriving ≈ 0.6–0.8', note: 'a mid-season satellite pass turns a 100-ha field into a mosaic — the raw material of zone maps.' },
    { math: 'nitrogen response: y = 4 + 0.05N − 0.0001N² (t/ha, N in kg/ha)', note: 'diminishing returns, fitted per zone from on-farm strip trials — the response curve IS the map\'s meaning.' },
    { math: 'economic optimum: last kg of N must return its own price', note: 'marginal yield × grain price = N price: (0.05 − 0.0002N) × 0.2 = 0.0012 → N = 220 kg/ha.' },
    { math: 'vs agronomic max N = 250 kg/ha', note: 'the last 30 kg/ha buys only ~0.09 t/ha ($18) and costs $36 — pure loss. Multiply the 30 kg across every hectare and the "waste curve" funds the sensor kit.' },
  ],
};

